{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Eviction for conviction, civil liability, theft, drug activity or human trafficking","requirement":"A landlord may file for eviction if the tenant is convicted of, pleads guilty to, or is held civilly liable for assault or threats against the landlord, family or employees, theft, drug activity or human trafficking; a three-day Notice to Quit is required before suit.","key_value":"3-day Notice to Quit; 2-year limit","coverage_conditions":"Tenants of leased premises, buildings, complexes and mobile home parks under the Eviction Law.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"Eviction based on the tenant's conviction, guilty plea, juvenile delinquency finding or civil liability for the listed offenses, or on permitting such a person to reside at the premises.","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"The landlord may file for eviction, if the tenant has been convicted of or pleaded guilty to, or if a juvenile has been found to be delinquent by the Court due to an offense involving theft of property from the landlord or from tenants residing in the same building or complex.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Eviction causes and notice for landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings","requirement":"A landlord-occupied two- or three-family tenant may be evicted only by court order and only for holding over, nonpayment, disorderly conduct, willful damage, constant rule violations or a lease violation reserving re-entry, with three-day to three-month notice to quit depending on cause and tenancy.","key_value":"3 days to 3 months notice to quit","coverage_conditions":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"owner","role":"covered","who":"landlord-occupied two- or three-family dwelling","unit_limit":3}],"per_tenancy":"Requires the landlord to prove one of the listed causes; the notice period depends on whether the tenancy is at will, year-to-year or month-to-month.","coverage_quotes":["Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings can be removed only when a court issues an order for eviction."]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2","quoted_span":"A three-day written Notice to Quit is required for any of the causes described as disorderly, destructive or violative of written rules or lease provisions (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2(a)).","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rooming and boarding house evictions: good cause and notice","requirement":"Owners of rooming and boarding houses must follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents and must give the County Welfare Board at least three working days' notice before starting an eviction action.","key_value":"3 working days' notice to the County Welfare Board","coverage_conditions":"Buildings with at least two living units occupied by unrelated persons without private kitchens and bathrooms, unless an exception in the Rooming and Boarding House Act applies.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"other","text":"At least two living units occupied by unrelated persons without private kitchens and bathrooms, unless an exception in the Rooming and Boarding House Act applies"}],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":["Any building having at least two (2) living units occupied by persons unrelated to each other without private kitchens and bathro oms is a rooming or boarding house"]},"exemptions":"The Bureau may order otherwise. Exceptions in the Rooming and Boarding House Act: hotels with more than 85 percent temporary occupancy, school and college dormitories, buildings housing only college students, and certain residences for the disabled.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3","quoted_span":"The Regulations Governing Rooming and Boarding Houses, which are enforced by the Bureau of Rooming and Boarding House Standards of the Department of Community Affairs, require owners of rooming and boarding houses to follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, except if otherwise ordered by the Bureau (N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)).","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Penalties for eviction law violations after owner-occupancy or demolition evictions","requirement":"A landlord who evicts for personal occupancy must occupy the unit at least six months, and a landlord who retires a unit from residential use may not use it residentially for five years. Violations bring treble damages, fees and up to a $10,000 civil penalty.","key_value":"6 months; 5 years; 3x damages; up to $10,000 civil penalty","coverage_conditions":"Tenants who vacate after notice that the landlord will personally occupy the unit or will permanently board up, demolish or retire the property from residential use.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"Applies where the tenant vacates after notice of the landlord's personal occupancy or of permanent removal of the property from residential use.","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Liable to the former tenant for three times the damages suffered plus attorney fees and costs; civil penalty up to $10,000.00 for each violation.","effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6","quoted_span":"Additionally, the landlord or former landlord may be liable for a civil penalty up to $10,000.00 for each violation of this law","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Reprisal against tenants who exercise civil rights","requirement":"A landlord may not evict, substantially alter the lease or refuse to renew in reprisal for a tenant enforcing lease or law rights, making a good faith health or safety complaint, organizing, or refusing retaliatory lease changes.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes, except owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"owner","role":"exempt","who":"owner-occupied two- or three-family dwelling","unit_limit":3}],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":["The law against reprisal applies to all rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes, except owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings."]},"exemptions":"Owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","penalty":"If a landlord does take reprisal action, the tenant may sue the landlord for damages in a civil action.","effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot take reprisal action against a tenant by eviction, substantial alteration of a lease or its terms, or refusal to renew a lease when a tenant exercises certain civil rights (N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10).","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act protection from conversion eviction","requirement":"Qualifying tenants who are at least 62 before the conversion recording, permanently disabled, or certain disabled veterans may be protected from eviction for 40 years if family income is not more than three times the county average per-person income or $50,000.","key_value":"40 years; 3x county average per-person income or $50,000, whichever is greater","coverage_conditions":"Tenants of buildings being converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership who meet the age, disability or veteran and income tests.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"Tenant must have lived in the building being converted at least one year before the conversion recording date and be at least 62, permanently disabled, or a qualifying disabled veteran, within the stated income limit.","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.22 to -61.39","quoted_span":"Tenants may be protected from eviction for 40 years if their family income is not more than three (3) times the average per person income in their county or $50,000.00","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant Protection Act of 1992 protected tenancy in qualified counties","requirement":"The 1992 amendment extends protected-tenancy protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties, at present only Hudson County, in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible under the Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted; at present only Hudson County is a qualified county.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"other","text":"Only qualified counties; at present only Hudson County"}],"per_tenancy":"Protection applies to tenants who were not eligible as senior citizens or disabled persons under the 1981 Act.","coverage_quotes":["At the present time, the only qualified county is Hudson County (N.J.A.C. 5:24-3.2(b))."]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59","quoted_span":"The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 amendment extends protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant protections after foreclosure","requirement":"Foreclosure alone is not grounds for eviction; a buyer who will personally occupy the foreclosed property must give a 90-day notice to quit, and a new owner must notify tenants in English and Spanish within 10 business days of the sale.","key_value":"90-day notice to quit; 10 business days","coverage_conditions":"Tenants of foreclosed properties containing one or more residential rental units; month-to-month tenants and two- and three-family owner-occupied units are not exempt from the 90-day notice.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"Applies to tenancies in a property that has been foreclosed; a lease running beyond 90 days protects the tenant until the lease expires and the 90-day notice has been given.","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:50-69 et seq.","quoted_span":"Any person acquiring a foreclosed property containing one or more residential rental units must provide notices to the tenants in English and Spanish, within 10 business days after the sale, letting tenants know that ownership has changed hands and that the tenants are not required to move because of the foreclosure.","interaction":"Federal foreclosure law does not preempt state or local laws giving tenants longer time periods or additional protections.","relations":[{"type":"yields_to_local","quote":"the federal law does not preempt any State or local law that provides longer time periods or other additional protections for tenants"}],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
